### COSTS
- read/write by index: O(1)
- push/pop at the end: O(1)
- insert/remove at the front or middle: O(n)
- search an unsorted array: O(n); a sorted one with binary search: O(log n)

### COMMON TECHNIQUES
- Two pointers from both ends (palindromes, pair sums in sorted arrays)
- Sliding window over a substring
- Frequency counting with a Map or a 26-slot array for lowercase letters
- Sort first if order doesn't matter: O(n log n) often unlocks O(n) logic afterwards
- In-place tricks: write pointer for "remove duplicates", reverse a range

### EXAMPLE: valid anagram (counting array)

```js
function isAnagram(s, t) {
    if (s.length !== t.length) return false;
    const counts = new Array(26).fill(0);
    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
        counts[s.charCodeAt(i) - 97]++;
        counts[t.charCodeAt(i) - 97]--;
    }
    return counts.every((c) => c === 0);
}
```

### PRACTICE
Easy: Contains Duplicate, Valid Anagram, Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock
Medium: Product of Array Except Self, Group Anagrams, Encode and Decode Strings, Rotate Image, Spiral Matrix

### WHY THEY MATTER
The most common optimisation in interviews: trade O(n) memory for O(1) lookups, turning O(n^2) into O(n).

### CLASSIC: Two Sum

```js
function twoSum(nums, target) {
    const indexOf = new Map(); // value -> index
    for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        const needed = target - nums[i];
        if (indexOf.has(needed)) return [indexOf.get(needed), i];
        indexOf.set(nums[i], i);
    }
    return [];
}
```

### USE THEM FOR
- "Have I seen this before?" -> Set
- Counting -> Map value -> count
- Grouping -> Map key -> array (e.g. sorted word -> list of anagrams)
- Caching results (memoisation in DP)

### GOTCHAS
- Arrays and objects as Map keys compare by reference. Build a string key instead: `${row},${col}`.
- Worst case is O(n) on collisions. In interviews, assume O(1) average and say "average case".

### PRACTICE
Easy: Two Sum, Ransom Note, Isomorphic Strings
Medium: Group Anagrams, Top K Frequent Elements, Longest Consecutive Sequence, Subarray Sum Equals K (with prefix sums)

### WHAT IT IS
Nodes that each point to the next node. O(1) insert/delete when you already hold the node, O(n) to find one.

```js
function ListNode(val, next = null) { this.val = val; this.next = next; }
```

![A singly linked list](images/singly-linked-list.svg)
_A singly linked list. Image: Lasindi, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons_

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph before [Before]
        A1[1] --> B1[2] --> C1[3] --> N1[null]
    end
    subgraph after [After reverseList]
        C2[3] --> B2[2] --> A2[1] --> N2[null]
    end
```

### TECHNIQUES
1. Dummy head: avoids special cases when the head changes.

```js
   const dummy = new ListNode(0, head);
```

2. Fast & slow pointers: find the middle, detect a cycle.
3. Reverse in place: the most-asked building block.

### REVERSE A LIST

```js
function reverseList(head) {
    let prev = null;
    let current = head;
    while (current) {
        const next = current.next; // remember the rest
        current.next = prev;       // flip the pointer
        prev = current;            // move both forward
        current = next;
    }
    return prev;
}
```

### CYCLE DETECTION (Floyd)

```js
let slow = head, fast = head;
while (fast && fast.next) {
    slow = slow.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
    if (slow === fast) return true;
}
return false;
```

### TIP
Draw boxes and arrows on paper before you code. Most linked-list bugs come from losing a reference.

### PRACTICE
Easy: Reverse Linked List, Merge Two Sorted Lists, Linked List Cycle
Medium: Reorder List, Remove Nth Node From End, Copy List with Random Pointer, Add Two Numbers, LRU Cache (Map + doubly linked list)
Hard: Merge K Sorted Lists, Reverse Nodes in K-Group

### STACK: last in, first out
JS array with push/pop: both O(1).
Use for: matching brackets, undo, evaluating expressions, DFS without recursion, monotonic stack problems.

```js
function isValid(s) {
    const pairs = { ')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{' };
    const stack = [];
    for (const ch of s) {
        if (pairs[ch]) {
            if (stack.pop() !== pairs[ch]) return false;
        } else {
            stack.push(ch);
        }
    }
    return stack.length === 0;
}
```

![Stack: push and pop at the same end](images/lifo-stack.png)
_Stack (last in, first out). Image: Maxtremus, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons_

![Queue: enqueue at the back, dequeue at the front](images/queue.svg)
_Queue (first in, first out). Image: Vegpuff, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons_

### QUEUE: first in, first out
Use for: BFS, processing tasks in order.
Don't use arr.shift() in a loop: it's O(n). Keep a head index instead:

```js
const queue = [start];
let head = 0;
while (head < queue.length) {
    const node = queue[head++];
    // ... push neighbours with queue.push(...)
}
```

### DEQUE (both ends)
Needed for "sliding window maximum". Use an array of indexes with a head pointer, popping from the back.

### PRACTICE
Easy: Valid Parentheses, Implement Queue using Stacks
Medium: Min Stack, Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation, Daily Temperatures, Car Fleet, Decode String
Hard: Largest Rectangle in Histogram, Sliding Window Maximum

### WHAT IT IS
A tree stored in an array where the parent is always &lt;= its children (min-heap). The smallest item is at index 0.
- peek: O(1)
- push / pop: O(log n)
- build from n items: O(n)

![A max-heap](images/max-heap.svg)
_A max-heap (parents are bigger than children; a min-heap is the reverse). Image: Ermishin, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons_

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["index 0 : 1"] --> B["index 1 : 3"]
    A --> C["index 2 : 5"]
    B --> D["index 3 : 7"]
    B --> E["index 4 : 9"]
    C --> F["index 5 : 8"]
```

The same min-heap stored as an array `[1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 8]`: the children of index `i` are at `2i + 1` and `2i + 2`, and the parent is at `Math.floor((i - 1) / 2)`.

### USE FOR
"K largest/smallest", "merge K sorted lists", "next task to run" (scheduling), Dijkstra's shortest path, running median (two heaps).

### JS HAS NO BUILT-IN HEAP: memorise this one

```js
class MinHeap {
    constructor(compare = (a, b) => a - b) { this.items = []; this.compare = compare; }
    size() { return this.items.length; }
    peek() { return this.items[0]; }
    push(value) {
        this.items.push(value);
        let i = this.items.length - 1;
        while (i > 0) {
            const parent = Math.floor((i - 1) / 2);
            if (this.compare(this.items[i], this.items[parent]) >= 0) break;
            [this.items[i], this.items[parent]] = [this.items[parent], this.items[i]];
            i = parent;
        }
    }
    pop() {
        const top = this.items[0];
        const last = this.items.pop();
        if (this.items.length > 0) {
            this.items[0] = last;
            let i = 0;
            while (true) {
                const left = 2 * i + 1, right = 2 * i + 2;
                let smallest = i;
                if (left < this.items.length && this.compare(this.items[left], this.items[smallest]) < 0) smallest = left;
                if (right < this.items.length && this.compare(this.items[right], this.items[smallest]) < 0) smallest = right;
                if (smallest === i) break;
                [this.items[i], this.items[smallest]] = [this.items[smallest], this.items[i]];
                i = smallest;
            }
        }
        return top;
    }
}
```

Max-heap: new MinHeap((a, b) => b - a)

### PRACTICE
Easy: Kth Largest Element in a Stream, Last Stone Weight
Medium: K Closest Points to Origin, Kth Largest Element in an Array, Task Scheduler, Top K Frequent Words
Hard: Find Median from Data Stream, Merge K Sorted Lists

### WHAT IT IS
A tree where each edge is one character. Looking up a word or prefix costs O(length of the word), whatever the number of stored words.

![A trie storing several words](images/trie.svg)
_A trie: words that share a prefix share a path. Image: Booyabazooka, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons_

### USE FOR
Autocomplete, "starts with" queries, word search on a board, spell-check.

### SIMPLE IMPLEMENTATION

```js
class Trie {
    constructor() { this.root = { children: {}, isWord: false }; }
    insert(word) {
        let node = this.root;
        for (const ch of word) {
            if (!node.children[ch]) node.children[ch] = { children: {}, isWord: false };
            node = node.children[ch];
        }
        node.isWord = true;
    }
    findNode(text) {
        let node = this.root;
        for (const ch of text) {
            node = node.children[ch];
            if (!node) return null;
        }
        return node;
    }
    search(word) { const node = this.findNode(word); return !!node && node.isWord; }
    startsWith(prefix) { return this.findNode(prefix) !== null; }
}
```

### REAL-WORLD LINK
A search box with autocomplete is a natural place to talk about tries in a system design interview.

### PRACTICE
Medium: Implement Trie, Design Add and Search Words Data Structure
Hard: Word Search II

### IDEA
Two indexes moving through the data, usually from both ends towards each other, or one fast and one slow. Turns many O(n^2) pair searches into O(n).

### WHEN TO REACH FOR IT
- The array is sorted (or you can sort it)
- "Find a pair/triplet that sums to X"
- Palindromes, reversing, merging two sorted arrays
- Removing items in place (read pointer + write pointer)

### TEMPLATE: pair sum in a sorted array

```js
function pairWithSum(sorted, target) {
    let left = 0;
    let right = sorted.length - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        const sum = sorted[left] + sorted[right];
        if (sum === target) return [left, right];
        if (sum < target) left++;   // need a bigger sum
        else right--;               // need a smaller sum
    }
    return null;
}
```

### 3SUM IN ONE SENTENCE
Sort, fix each number i, run the two-pointer search on the rest, and skip duplicates. O(n^2).

### PRACTICE
Easy: Valid Palindrome, Two Sum II, Merge Sorted Array, Move Zeroes
Medium: 3Sum, Container With Most Water, Sort Colors
Hard: Trapping Rain Water

### IDEA
For contiguous subarrays or substrings: keep a window [left, right], grow it on the right, and shrink it from the left when it becomes invalid. Each element enters and leaves once, so it's O(n).

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    G[Move right one step and add its item] --> V{Window still valid?}
    V -- yes --> R[Update best answer]
    V -- no --> S[Remove the item at left, move left one step]
    S --> V
    R --> E{right at the end?}
    E -- no --> G
    E -- yes --> D[Done]
```

### WHEN TO REACH FOR IT
"Longest/shortest substring/subarray such that...", "at most K distinct", "contains all characters of...", "maximum sum of size K".

### TEMPLATE: variable-size window

```js
function longestSubstringWithoutRepeats(s) {
    const lastSeen = new Map(); // char -> last index
    let left = 0;
    let best = 0;
    for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
        const ch = s[right];
        if (lastSeen.has(ch) && lastSeen.get(ch) >= left) {
            left = lastSeen.get(ch) + 1; // jump past the repeat
        }
        lastSeen.set(ch, right);
        best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
    }
    return best;
}
```

### GENERIC SHAPE

```text
for right in 0..n-1:
    add s[right] to the window state
    while the window is invalid:
        remove s[left] from the state; left++
    update the answer
```

### PRACTICE
Easy: Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock, Maximum Average Subarray I
Medium: Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters, Longest Repeating Character Replacement, Permutation in String, Fruit Into Baskets
Hard: Minimum Window Substring, Sliding Window Maximum

### IDEA
Repeatedly halve a sorted search space. O(log n).

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    S["lo = 0, hi = n - 1"] --> L{"lo still at most hi?"}
    L -- no --> R[Return answer]
    L -- yes --> M["mid = floor((lo + hi) / 2)"]
    M --> C{"condition(mid)?"}
    C -- true --> T["answer = mid, hi = mid - 1"]
    C -- false --> F["lo = mid + 1"]
    T --> L
    F --> L
```

### SAFE TEMPLATE: first index where condition(i) is true

```js
function firstTrue(lo, hi, condition) {
    // search space is [lo, hi]; returns hi + 1 if never true
    let answer = hi + 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
        if (condition(mid)) {
            answer = mid;   // mid works, look further left
            hi = mid - 1;
        } else {
            lo = mid + 1;
        }
    }
    return answer;
}
// Insert position: firstTrue(0, nums.length - 1, (i) => nums[i] >= target)
```

### BINARY SEARCH ON THE ANSWER (a senior favourite)
When the question is "the minimum X that works" and "if X works, any bigger X also works", binary search over X.
Example, Koko Eating Bananas: search speed 1..max(pile); condition = "can finish in h hours at this speed".

### WATCH OUT FOR
- Infinite loops: always move lo or hi past mid.
- Rotated sorted arrays: one half is always sorted; decide which side the target is on.

### PRACTICE
Easy: Binary Search, Search Insert Position
Medium: Search a 2D Matrix, Koko Eating Bananas, Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array, Search in Rotated Sorted Array, Time Based Key-Value Store
Hard: Median of Two Sorted Arrays

### IDEA
Precompute running totals so any range sum is one subtraction.
prefix[i] = sum of nums[0..i-1]
sum(i..j) = prefix[j + 1] - prefix[i]

```js
const prefix = [0];
for (const x of nums) prefix.push(prefix[prefix.length - 1] + x);
```

### THE BIG ONE: count subarrays summing to K (works with negatives)

```js
function subarraySum(nums, k) {
    const seen = new Map([[0, 1]]); // prefix sum -> how many times seen
    let running = 0;
    let count = 0;
    for (const x of nums) {
        running += x;
        count += seen.get(running - k) || 0;
        seen.set(running, (seen.get(running) || 0) + 1);
    }
    return count;
}
```

Sliding window fails here when there are negative numbers. Prefix sums + a hash map is the fix, and recognising this is a strong signal.

### VARIANTS
- Prefix products (Product of Array Except Self)
- 2D prefix sums for sub-rectangle sums
- Difference arrays for "add X to a range" updates

### PRACTICE
Easy: Range Sum Query - Immutable, Running Sum of 1d Array
Medium: Subarray Sum Equals K, Product of Array Except Self, Continuous Subarray Sum, Range Sum Query 2D

### IDEA
A stack kept in increasing or decreasing order. When a new item breaks the order, pop items, and each pop has just found its "next greater/smaller element". O(n) overall, because each item is pushed and popped once.

### WHEN TO REACH FOR IT
"Next greater element", "how many days until a warmer temperature", "largest rectangle", "stock span".

### TEMPLATE: Daily Temperatures

```js
function dailyTemperatures(temps) {
    const answer = new Array(temps.length).fill(0);
    const stack = []; // indexes of days still waiting for a warmer day
    for (let i = 0; i < temps.length; i++) {
        while (stack.length && temps[i] > temps[stack[stack.length - 1]]) {
            const day = stack.pop();
            answer[day] = i - day;
        }
        stack.push(i);
    }
    return answer;
}
```

### TIP
Store INDEXES on the stack, not values. You almost always need the distance or position.

### PRACTICE
Medium: Daily Temperatures, Next Greater Element II, Online Stock Span, Car Fleet, Remove K Digits
Hard: Largest Rectangle in Histogram, Maximal Rectangle

### DFS (depth-first): go deep, then backtrack. Recursion or an explicit stack.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A((A)) --> B((B))
    A --> C((C))
    B --> D((D))
    B --> E((E))
    C --> F((F))
    C --> G((G))
```

- **BFS** (level by level): A, B, C, D, E, F, G
- **DFS** pre-order (go deep first): A, B, D, E, C, F, G

### BFS (breadth-first): go level by level with a queue. Finds the SHORTEST path when every step costs the same.

### DFS ON A GRID: count islands

```js
function numIslands(grid) {
    const rows = grid.length, cols = grid[0].length;
    let islands = 0;
    function sink(r, c) {
        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= rows || c >= cols || grid[r][c] !== '1') return;
        grid[r][c] = '0'; // mark visited
        sink(r + 1, c); sink(r - 1, c); sink(r, c + 1); sink(r, c - 1);
    }
    for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
        for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
            if (grid[r][c] === '1') { islands++; sink(r, c); }
        }
    }
    return islands;
}
```

### BFS: shortest number of steps

```js
function shortestSteps(start, isGoal, getNeighbours) {
    const visited = new Set([start]);
    let level = [start];
    let steps = 0;
    while (level.length) {
        const next = [];
        for (const node of level) {
            if (isGoal(node)) return steps;
            for (const n of getNeighbours(node)) {
                if (!visited.has(n)) { visited.add(n); next.push(n); }
            }
        }
        level = next;
        steps++;
    }
    return -1;
}
```

Processing a level array at a time avoids queue.shift() and makes "steps" easy to count.

### MULTI-SOURCE BFS
Put every starting point in the first level (e.g. all rotten oranges). Each round is one minute.

### RECURSION DEPTH
Deep DFS on a 1000x1000 grid can overflow the JS call stack. Mention it, and switch to an explicit stack if needed.

### PRACTICE
Medium: Number of Islands, Rotting Oranges, Walls and Gates, Surrounded Regions, Shortest Path in Binary Matrix, Course Schedule
Hard: Word Ladder

### WHAT IT IS
Recursion plus remembering answers. If a problem breaks into overlapping sub-problems and the best answer is built from best sub-answers, DP applies.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    F5["f(5)"] --> F4["f(4)"]
    F5 --> F3a["f(3)"]
    F4 --> F3b["f(3)"]
    F4 --> F2a["f(2)"]
    F3a --> F2b["f(2)"]
    F3a --> F1a["f(1)"]
    F3b --> F2c["f(2)"]
    F3b --> F1b["f(1)"]
    classDef repeat fill:#7a3b3b,stroke:#e06c6c,color:#fff
    class F3a,F3b,F2a,F2b,F2c repeat
```

The red calls are repeated work. A memo stores each answer the first time, so every `f(n)` is computed once.

### SIGNALS
"How many ways...", "minimum/maximum cost...", "is it possible to...", with choices at each step.

### THE 4-STEP RECIPE
1. Define the state in words: "dp[i] = the fewest coins needed to make amount i".
2. Write the recurrence: dp[i] = 1 + min(dp[i - coin]) for each coin.
3. Base cases: dp[0] = 0.
4. Order: compute small i before big i (bottom-up), or recurse with a memo (top-down).

### TOP-DOWN (memo): often easiest to think of first

```js
function climbStairs(n, memo = new Map()) {
    if (n <= 2) return n;
    if (memo.has(n)) return memo.get(n);
    const ways = climbStairs(n - 1, memo) + climbStairs(n - 2, memo);
    memo.set(n, ways);
    return ways;
}
```

### BOTTOM-UP (table): no recursion limit

```js
function coinChange(coins, amount) {
    const dp = new Array(amount + 1).fill(Infinity);
    dp[0] = 0;
    for (let i = 1; i <= amount; i++) {
        for (const coin of coins) {
            if (coin <= i) dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], dp[i - coin] + 1);
        }
    }
    return dp[amount] === Infinity ? -1 : dp[amount];
}
```

### INTERVIEW TIP
Say the brute-force recursion first, point out the repeated sub-problems, then add the memo. Interviewers love seeing that progression.

### PRACTICE
Easy: Climbing Stairs, Min Cost Climbing Stairs
Medium: House Robber, House Robber II, Coin Change, Decode Ways, Word Break, Maximum Product Subarray

### GREEDY
Make the locally best choice at each step and never revisit it. Fast and simple, but only correct when you can argue why the local choice is safe. Be ready to give a one-sentence reason.

Example, Jump Game: track the furthest index reachable so far.

```js
function canJump(nums) {
    let furthest = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        if (i > furthest) return false;
        furthest = Math.max(furthest, i + nums[i]);
    }
    return true;
}
```

### INTERVALS: almost always "sort by start first"

```js
function merge(intervals) {
    intervals.sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
    const merged = [intervals[0]];
    for (const [start, end] of intervals.slice(1)) {
        const last = merged[merged.length - 1];
        if (start <= last[1]) last[1] = Math.max(last[1], end); // overlap
        else merged.push([start, end]);
    }
    return merged;
}
```

### INTERVAL VARIANTS
- Fewest removals to stop overlaps: sort by END and keep the earliest-ending ones.
- Meeting rooms needed: sort the starts and ends separately, or use a min-heap of end times.

### PRACTICE
Medium: Jump Game, Jump Game II, Gas Station, Hand of Straights, Merge Intervals, Insert Interval, Non-overlapping Intervals, Meeting Rooms II, Partition Labels

### IDEA
Tracks which items are in the same group, and merges groups in nearly O(1). Great for "are these connected?" and counting components while edges arrive.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph before ["Before union(2, 4)"]
        direction BT
        b1((1)) --> b0((0))
        b2((2)) --> b0
        b4((4)) --> b3((3))
    end
    subgraph after ["After union(2, 4)"]
        direction BT
        a1((1)) --> a0((0))
        a2((2)) --> a0
        a3((3)) --> a0
        a4((4)) --> a3
    end
```

Each node points to its parent; the root names the group. `union` hangs the smaller tree under the bigger one's root.

### IMPLEMENTATION (path compression + union by size)

```js
class UnionFind {
    constructor(n) {
        this.parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i);
        this.size = new Array(n).fill(1);
        this.groups = n;
    }
    find(x) {
        while (this.parent[x] !== x) {
            this.parent[x] = this.parent[this.parent[x]]; // shortcut the path
            x = this.parent[x];
        }
        return x;
    }
    union(a, b) {
        let rootA = this.find(a), rootB = this.find(b);
        if (rootA === rootB) return false; // already connected: this edge would make a cycle
        if (this.size[rootA] < this.size[rootB]) [rootA, rootB] = [rootB, rootA];
        this.parent[rootB] = rootA;
        this.size[rootA] += this.size[rootB];
        this.groups--;
        return true;
    }
}
```

### USE FOR
Number of connected components, redundant connection (cycle detection in an undirected graph), accounts merge, Kruskal's minimum spanning tree.

### PRACTICE
Medium: Number of Connected Components in an Undirected Graph, Redundant Connection, Graph Valid Tree, Accounts Merge, Number of Provinces
Hard: Min Cost to Connect All Points (Kruskal)

### IDEA
Order tasks so every task comes after its prerequisites. Only possible if there's no cycle. Think build systems, course schedules, package installs, database migrations.

![A directed acyclic graph](images/directed-acyclic-graph.svg)
_A directed acyclic graph (no cycles), so it has a topological order. Image: Johannes Rössel, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons_

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    P[Intro to programming] --> DS[Data structures]
    DM[Discrete maths] --> AL[Algorithms]
    DS --> AL
    AL --> SD[System design]
```

One valid order: Intro to programming, Discrete maths, Data structures, Algorithms, System design.

### KAHN'S ALGORITHM (BFS on in-degrees)

```js
function topoOrder(numNodes, edges) { // edges: [before, after]
    const graph = Array.from({ length: numNodes }, () => []);
    const inDegree = new Array(numNodes).fill(0);
    for (const [before, after] of edges) {
        graph[before].push(after);
        inDegree[after]++;
    }
    const ready = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < numNodes; i++) if (inDegree[i] === 0) ready.push(i);

    const order = [];
    for (let head = 0; head < ready.length; head++) {
        const node = ready[head];
        order.push(node);
        for (const next of graph[node]) {
            inDegree[next]--;
            if (inDegree[next] === 0) ready.push(next);
        }
    }
    return order.length === numNodes ? order : null; // null means a cycle
}
```

