### WHAT TO COVER FOR EVERY ENDPOINT
Happy path; validation errors (400/422); not logged in (401); someone else's data (403/404); not found; method not allowed (405); and the response shape.

### CONTRACT TESTING
When a frontend and backend (or two services) are deployed separately, contract tests catch breaking API changes without running both together.
- Consumer-driven (Pact): the consumer's tests record "I call GET /users/1 and need `id` and `email`"; the provider's build replays those expectations against the real API.
- Schema-based: validate responses against the OpenAPI spec in tests.

### MOCKING THE NETWORK IN FRONTEND TESTS: MSW
Mock Service Worker intercepts `fetch` at the network level, so your app code stays untouched:
```js
import { http, HttpResponse } from 'msw';
import { setupServer } from 'msw/node';

const server = setupServer(
  http.get('/api/notes', () => HttpResponse.json({ notes: [] })),
);
beforeAll(() => server.listen());
afterEach(() => server.resetHandlers());
afterAll(() => server.close());

// in one test: simulate a failure
server.use(http.get('/api/notes', () => new HttpResponse(null, { status: 500 })));
```
The same handlers work in unit tests, Storybook and in the browser during development (backend not ready yet).

